> There's the option of not playing non-optimally, which a number of
> players currently do in the vast majority of cases.  Assuming your
> players will _always_ play non-optimally is a mistake; even a poor
> player does the right thing on occasion.  (cf. blind squirrel)

You can also make the "optimal" approach less clear. One of the things
I don't like about NetHack 3.4.3 is that the optimal approach has little
to do with class, race, or the equipment you find in the early game;
Spork mixes this up a little and other games mix it up a lot (though 
mixing it up a lot comes with different problems). 

(And yes, certainly NetHack has some variety here; one of the things
I like about the Wizard early game is that your playstyle changes in
response to the spellbooks you find. In contrast, Barbarian or Valkyrie
playstyle does not change in response to the weapons you find, with the
possible exception of your weapon slot strategy changing if you find a
very powerful artifact weapon on the ground.)

